1
00:00:14,030 --> 00:00:16,431
[SPEAKER_01]: Why ill-will and lust, exactly?

2
00:00:16,471 --> 00:00:17,831
[SPEAKER_05]: Why do they always go hand in hand?

3
00:00:17,951 --> 00:00:20,552
[SPEAKER_05]: Why, when there is lust, there will be ill-will?

4
00:00:24,733 --> 00:00:33,015
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, when there's a dependency on lust, which is always directed towards things in the world, with that comes the possibility that other people might get in your way.

5
00:00:33,396 --> 00:00:41,678
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, but even more acutely, from your point of view, so, that dependency on lust, how is that maintained?

6
00:00:42,444 --> 00:00:45,105
[SPEAKER_05]: How do you perpetuate your own dependency on lust?

7
00:00:46,486 --> 00:00:47,226
[SPEAKER_05]: By being lustful.

8
00:00:48,147 --> 00:00:50,088
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, but how are you being lustful?

9
00:00:50,168 --> 00:00:54,250
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, by not seeing the danger in being lustful, by not knowing the escape from that.

10
00:00:55,771 --> 00:01:05,475
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, so a person doesn't know the escape, but can find the escape, by not doing certain things, which, if you're doing, you're keeping your dependence on lust.

11
00:01:05,695 --> 00:01:05,876
[SPEAKER_05]: Yes.

12
00:01:06,236 --> 00:01:10,698
[SPEAKER_05]: So, what are those things that you should talk about, that you shouldn't do, while you're still dependent on lust?

13
00:01:12,437 --> 00:01:15,358
[SPEAKER_04]: Oh, what you should not do is attend to things that are provocative of lust.

14
00:01:15,778 --> 00:01:16,179
[SPEAKER_05]: Exactly.

15
00:01:16,399 --> 00:01:29,064
[SPEAKER_05]: So, okay, virtue, sense, strength, but then you should not be grasping the features of beautiful that are increasing the lust, increasing the wanting, increasing the sensuality.

16
00:01:29,944 --> 00:01:30,685
[SPEAKER_05]: And it's because

17
00:01:31,644 --> 00:01:46,581
[SPEAKER_05]: If you, unchecked, are constantly grasping the features of beautiful, let your mind dwell on them, want them, delight in them, welcome them, you will be unable to help yourself but grasp the features of ugly, when you experience the disagreeable experience.

18
00:01:47,202 --> 00:01:50,025
[SPEAKER_05]: In other words, you will be grasping the features of hate.

19
00:01:51,297 --> 00:01:52,518
[SPEAKER_05]: When the experience changes.

20
00:01:53,279 --> 00:02:05,571
[SPEAKER_05]: So, if you meet somebody who is not a lovely person and who is quite unpleasant, your mind will not be able to not pick the features of resistance to that and anger and everything else that will come out of it.

21
00:02:05,791 --> 00:02:07,713
[SPEAKER_04]: Because it's operating on the level of science.

22
00:02:07,733 --> 00:02:10,135
[SPEAKER_05]: Because the mind operates on picking up the features.

23
00:02:10,475 --> 00:02:10,696
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah.

24
00:02:11,652 --> 00:02:17,336
[SPEAKER_05]: It has not discerned the bigger picture and learned how not to pick the features and signs and features of beautiful.

25
00:02:17,516 --> 00:02:21,519
[SPEAKER_05]: So, there will be signs and features of beautiful waiting, but you don't need to pick them up.

26
00:02:21,840 --> 00:02:25,963
[SPEAKER_05]: But you can't not pick them up, because all you know is acting towards sensuality.

27
00:02:27,183 --> 00:02:27,404
[SPEAKER_05]: Same.

28
00:02:27,424 --> 00:02:37,111
[SPEAKER_05]: There will be features of ugly and unpleasant, but you can't not pick them up and get all wound up and angry, because you keep acting towards sense objects.

29
00:02:39,012 --> 00:02:39,773
[SPEAKER_05]: But you're not angry.

30
00:02:40,974 --> 00:02:48,818
[SPEAKER_05]: So, you can wish, or if I cannot get angry now, well, you can achieve that by not being sensual either when you're not angry.

31
00:02:49,259 --> 00:02:56,002
[SPEAKER_02]: If you haven't understood it correctly, you will still be blaming that on the situation not being good enough.

32
00:02:56,042 --> 00:02:56,823
[SPEAKER_05]: Well, that's what it means.

33
00:02:56,923 --> 00:02:58,043
[SPEAKER_05]: That's what sensuality means.

34
00:02:58,083 --> 00:02:59,064
[SPEAKER_05]: That's what the ill-will means.

35
00:02:59,204 --> 00:03:05,788
[SPEAKER_05]: It means seeing past where the problem actually is and assuming it on the sense objects.

36
00:03:07,600 --> 00:03:11,201
[SPEAKER_05]: The last, the delight, the gratification is not in the sense of objects.

37
00:03:11,301 --> 00:03:13,582
[SPEAKER_05]: It's in your happiness and delight on account of it.

38
00:03:13,702 --> 00:03:15,563
[SPEAKER_05]: But you see way, way past that.

39
00:03:15,583 --> 00:03:17,484
[SPEAKER_05]: Immediately, you're down into the objects.

40
00:03:18,724 --> 00:03:27,067
[SPEAKER_05]: Equally, when it's unpleasant, the pain, the discomfort of it, it's not because of some hateful word you're experiencing and so on.

41
00:03:27,787 --> 00:03:29,148
[SPEAKER_05]: It's because your mind's resisting it.

42
00:03:29,208 --> 00:03:30,649
[SPEAKER_05]: But you don't see that level.

43
00:03:30,689 --> 00:03:33,990
[SPEAKER_05]: You're way past it, onto the face of the person that's annoying you.

44
00:03:37,033 --> 00:03:51,561
[SPEAKER_05]: So, through cultivating sensuality, not practicing sense restraint, you are cultivating your mind of overlooking where the problem is, not seeing that domain.

45
00:03:51,702 --> 00:03:53,483
[SPEAKER_05]: That is all there is to it.

46
00:03:54,103 --> 00:04:02,348
[SPEAKER_05]: But in order to see that level, in order to be able to recognize it, endure it sufficiently until you see your intentions in regard to it,

47
00:04:03,057 --> 00:04:12,122
[SPEAKER_05]: You have to have, out of faith or some sort of pondering and understanding, withdrawn yourself from acting out towards sense objects, beautiful or ugly.

48
00:04:12,142 --> 00:04:13,022
[SPEAKER_05]: So, precepts.

49
00:04:13,723 --> 00:04:20,906
[SPEAKER_05]: Acting towards sensuality, acting out of anger, ill-will, hatred, bad speech, all of that needs to have been honed in and restrained.

50
00:04:25,489 --> 00:04:27,150
[SPEAKER_05]: So, in other words, it's impossible to not

51
00:04:28,578 --> 00:04:43,406
[SPEAKER_05]: To be able to not grasp the signs and features and overlook where the problem is in regard to sensuality or real will, if you're still not withdrawn from it with your body, if you're still acting out and seeking.

52
00:04:43,606 --> 00:04:45,927
[SPEAKER_03]: It's called things that entail suffering.

53
00:04:46,848 --> 00:04:47,568
[SPEAKER_03]: Dukkha dhamma.

54
00:04:48,409 --> 00:04:49,049
[SPEAKER_03]: Dukkha dhammas.

55
00:04:50,830 --> 00:04:54,292
[SPEAKER_03]: That's got like a few questions, which we can stop at and see.

56
00:04:56,173 --> 00:05:16,817
[SPEAKER_03]: It says, when a bhikkhu completely understands the comings and goings, as I just translated that origin and cessation, when he completely understands the comings and goings of things that entail suffering, then sensual pleasures have been seen by him in such a way that as he looks at them,

57
00:05:19,960 --> 00:05:33,679
[SPEAKER_03]: Then, he has comprehended a way of living whereby evil unwholesome states of greed, lust and sadness or displeasure do not flow in upon him.

58
00:05:36,832 --> 00:05:43,736
[SPEAKER_03]: So, that's the first statement, and it's just broken up into a few parts.

59
00:05:44,356 --> 00:05:53,101
[SPEAKER_03]: The first question is, and how does a bhikkhu completely understand the comings and goings of things that entail suffering?

60
00:06:00,565 --> 00:06:01,425
[SPEAKER_03]: How is that understood?

61
00:06:01,625 --> 00:06:02,106
[SPEAKER_03]: Any ideas?

62
00:06:04,867 --> 00:06:06,088
[SPEAKER_02]: Comings and goings is that

63
00:06:07,096 --> 00:06:07,976
[SPEAKER_03]: It's not a trick question.

64
00:06:07,996 --> 00:06:10,357
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, I know, but I'm wondering...

65
00:06:11,397 --> 00:06:12,558
[SPEAKER_02]: Rising and falling.

66
00:06:12,578 --> 00:06:14,118
[SPEAKER_02]: Upādāvaya.

67
00:06:14,138 --> 00:06:21,161
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, just how it's... And the translation is like how it's produced and how it falls apart, basically.

68
00:06:22,261 --> 00:06:24,742
[SPEAKER_03]: How do you... How do you know that?

69
00:06:26,982 --> 00:06:28,063
[SPEAKER_03]: How do you understand that?

70
00:06:33,346 --> 00:06:44,398
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, to endure, without interfering or acting out of them.

71
00:06:44,579 --> 00:06:54,510
[SPEAKER_03]: There's one, and I would say, it doesn't really say it there, but it does say, such is form.

72
00:06:55,372 --> 00:06:58,013
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, I was going to say, what are the things that entail suffering?

73
00:06:58,454 --> 00:06:59,834
[SPEAKER_03]: It's like, this is form.

74
00:07:00,515 --> 00:07:01,455
[SPEAKER_03]: This is its origin.

75
00:07:01,675 --> 00:07:02,736
[SPEAKER_03]: This is its cessation.

76
00:07:03,256 --> 00:07:06,598
[SPEAKER_03]: So, to me, that's saying... And how does he understand things?

77
00:07:06,618 --> 00:07:12,140
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, he investigates it.

78
00:07:12,160 --> 00:07:21,865
[SPEAKER_05]: The problem there is, though, that one can investigate and get the right answers, but still on the abstract level.

79
00:07:23,468 --> 00:07:24,389
[SPEAKER_04]: Oh, yeah, such is form.

80
00:07:24,509 --> 00:07:27,832
[SPEAKER_05]: I mean, the whole commentarial texts were written on what the form is.

81
00:07:27,912 --> 00:07:29,493
[SPEAKER_05]: It's made up of this.

82
00:07:29,813 --> 00:07:30,874
[SPEAKER_05]: Made up of that.

83
00:07:30,934 --> 00:07:33,216
[SPEAKER_05]: Okay, I got information thus this is the form.

84
00:07:33,296 --> 00:07:34,998
[SPEAKER_05]: But no, it starts with endurance.

85
00:07:35,979 --> 00:07:51,732
[SPEAKER_05]: By not acting out of the form that pressures you, not acting out of the concern in regard to your form that you are pressured by, by discerning the nature of it enduring there, on its own, you're paired with it, then you get to see such is the form.

86
00:07:52,399 --> 00:07:53,374
[SPEAKER_05]: Like, you might not know,

87
00:07:53,769 --> 00:07:56,570
[SPEAKER_05]: You have the information how the form came about, and you don't need to.

88
00:07:57,190 --> 00:07:59,871
[SPEAKER_05]: You can still understand the aggregate of form.

89
00:08:00,311 --> 00:08:23,298
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, but having it endure, you know, such is form, and then you start to let it, well, you let it endure, then you get to see all these other... Yeah, but that needs to be qualified, because, as I said, people already think they can practice the dhamma without a right view, means they can think that all this information you get and you learn about the first, second, fourth jhāna, the five aggregates, all of that applies to them already, but it doesn't.

90
00:08:24,181 --> 00:08:28,022
[SPEAKER_05]: In order for it to apply, you need to create a basis for which to apply.

91
00:08:28,563 --> 00:08:29,423
[SPEAKER_05]: And that's the basis.

92
00:08:30,203 --> 00:08:37,646
[SPEAKER_05]: Precepts, guiding of the sense doors, the mind, purification, development on account of it, and then, yeah, such is the form that endures.

93
00:08:37,806 --> 00:08:46,829
[SPEAKER_05]: Because, see, even people, they would then be finding out what is the form, where it comes from, why is it here, how is it here, what created it, what will be its end.

94
00:08:47,549 --> 00:08:48,530
[SPEAKER_05]: All of those questions,

95
00:08:49,308 --> 00:08:51,050
[SPEAKER_05]: would be rooted in trying to get rid of the form.

96
00:08:51,330 --> 00:08:54,573
[SPEAKER_05]: So, they don't understand what form is, because they don't want to endure it, because it's unpleasant.

97
00:08:55,213 --> 00:08:56,895
[SPEAKER_05]: The pressure is uncomfortable.

98
00:08:57,255 --> 00:08:59,698
[SPEAKER_05]: So, I'm practicing the dhamma just to get rid of it.

99
00:09:00,518 --> 00:09:03,261
[SPEAKER_05]: So, the understanding begins by not acting out on that level.

100
00:09:04,546 --> 00:09:05,846
[SPEAKER_05]: And then you will know what form is.

101
00:09:06,046 --> 00:09:06,587
[SPEAKER_05]: Form is there.

102
00:09:06,987 --> 00:09:14,529
[SPEAKER_05]: And as it says in other suttas, he doesn't care whether it's far or near, personal, impersonal, up, down, left, right, past, future.

103
00:09:14,689 --> 00:09:17,290
[SPEAKER_05]: He knows the aggregator form as aggregator form.

104
00:09:17,510 --> 00:09:17,870
[SPEAKER_05]: It's there.

105
00:09:18,050 --> 00:09:18,911
[SPEAKER_05]: That's all I need to know.

106
00:09:19,531 --> 00:09:21,451
[SPEAKER_05]: Within that, then, you can actually understand.

107
00:09:21,711 --> 00:09:21,992
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yeah.

108
00:09:22,892 --> 00:09:23,992
[SPEAKER_05]: That's how form is.

109
00:09:24,472 --> 00:09:25,493
[SPEAKER_05]: That's how it increases.

110
00:09:25,673 --> 00:09:26,673
[SPEAKER_05]: That's how it decreases.

111
00:09:27,173 --> 00:09:33,015
[SPEAKER_05]: The entire extent of it has been abandoned by me because I'm not acting out of it anymore.

112
00:09:33,362 --> 00:09:34,483
[SPEAKER_05]: I'm not trying to get rid of it.

113
00:09:34,983 --> 00:09:36,324
[SPEAKER_05]: I'm not seeking the pleasure through it.

114
00:09:41,666 --> 00:09:46,049
[SPEAKER_05]: And then, same goes for the feeling, perception, intentions and consciousness.

115
00:09:55,894 --> 00:09:57,835
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you can get all that information, yeah.

116
00:09:58,475 --> 00:10:01,417
[SPEAKER_03]: But then, the result should be

117
00:10:04,092 --> 00:10:09,287
[SPEAKER_03]: That when sensual pleasures

118
00:10:10,419 --> 00:10:14,501
[SPEAKER_03]: When there are sensual pleasures available, he should have no desire.

119
00:10:14,881 --> 00:10:15,101
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

120
00:10:15,481 --> 00:10:21,844
[SPEAKER_03]: There, there's an infatuation for it, because he's understood the form, he's understood the feeling, so...

121
00:10:23,064 --> 00:10:28,026
[SPEAKER_03]: So, you've got all this information, a vast amount of it, but there's still that.

122
00:10:28,066 --> 00:10:34,189
[SPEAKER_03]: So, yeah, that should be an indication that you've... you just have information that's... that doesn't apply to you.

123
00:10:34,689 --> 00:10:35,509
[SPEAKER_03]: You're not applying it.

124
00:10:36,110 --> 00:10:37,090
[SPEAKER_03]: You're not seeing it rightly.

125
00:10:37,325 --> 00:10:52,912
[SPEAKER_02]: You can't understand it without seeing... without seeing the escape from it, basically, or without having the passion in regard to its... diminish or not... not being able to... at the same time.

126
00:10:56,294 --> 00:10:57,134
[SPEAKER_02]: Very incompatible.

127
00:10:57,634 --> 00:11:01,496
[SPEAKER_03]: Vipassana, gratification, danger, escape.

128
00:11:08,060 --> 00:11:17,243
[SPEAKER_03]: And how are sensual pleasures seen by him in such a way that as he looks at them, sensual desire does not lie latent within him?

129
00:11:21,704 --> 00:11:23,485
[SPEAKER_03]: So, how are sensual pleasures seen?

130
00:11:24,225 --> 00:11:24,945
[SPEAKER_04]: As impermanent.

131
00:11:28,406 --> 00:11:29,687
[SPEAKER_04]: That's why they are unsatisfying.

132
00:11:29,707 --> 00:11:30,427
[SPEAKER_04]: They don't last.

133
00:11:32,182 --> 00:11:38,647
[SPEAKER_03]: So, he sees them as unsatisfying, impermanent unsatisfaction, but also...

134
00:11:39,088 --> 00:11:39,328
[SPEAKER_05]: Ugly.

135
00:11:39,968 --> 00:11:43,291
[SPEAKER_05]: You've got to be seeing them as actually not attractive.

136
00:11:44,092 --> 00:11:54,700
[SPEAKER_05]: But, as I said before many times, you won't see that if your only concern is to act out towards more pleasure or get rid of the pain of not acting out towards pleasure.

137
00:11:56,261 --> 00:12:00,084
[SPEAKER_05]: I must replace this with a subha, subha, subha, because subha bothers me too much.

138
00:12:01,152 --> 00:12:10,578
[SPEAKER_05]: While you're looking at subha, as the sutta says, while you're looking at the beautiful, while he sees it, he is not attracted to it anymore.

139
00:12:11,499 --> 00:12:14,841
[SPEAKER_05]: There is no underlying desire and lust that can overwhelm the mind.

140
00:12:14,961 --> 00:12:15,241
[SPEAKER_05]: Why?

141
00:12:15,621 --> 00:12:18,583
[SPEAKER_05]: Because while he sees it, he sees the ugly within it.

142
00:12:18,763 --> 00:12:20,004
[SPEAKER_05]: Because that's what truly is.

143
00:12:20,665 --> 00:12:25,488
[SPEAKER_05]: If it weren't so, freedom from sensuality would not be possible, nor sensuality would be a problem.

144
00:12:26,628 --> 00:12:30,451
[SPEAKER_05]: But sensuality is a problem because it's a contradiction, it's a bait, it's a trap.

145
00:12:34,012 --> 00:12:35,412
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, it's not understood.

146
00:12:35,592 --> 00:12:37,753
[SPEAKER_05]: It's not understood for what it really is.

147
00:12:38,853 --> 00:12:40,653
[SPEAKER_05]: And why it's not understood for what it really is?

148
00:12:41,294 --> 00:12:47,555
[SPEAKER_05]: Because the mind is so used to, and has been doing it for so long, just picks the beautiful.

149
00:12:49,095 --> 00:13:00,117
[SPEAKER_05]: Like, imagine you have a bowl of just food and rubbish and dirt, everything just mixed in it, with some pieces that actually taste nice.

150
00:13:01,098 --> 00:13:02,578
[SPEAKER_05]: But it's all part of the same mix.

151
00:13:03,456 --> 00:13:04,416
[SPEAKER_05]: Well, that's sensuality.

152
00:13:04,717 --> 00:13:14,201
[SPEAKER_05]: Just focusing on the bits that taste nice and ignoring where it comes from and what it remains while you're eating it.

153
00:13:14,221 --> 00:13:17,182
[SPEAKER_05]: So, you have to stop ignoring it while it is there.

154
00:13:19,440 --> 00:13:34,049
[SPEAKER_05]: And that's why I keep saying, you can't just... yes, you have to be restrained and not just carelessly engage with thoughts of sensuality, but at the same time, you have to recognize that those thoughts are real as such, pressures are real as such, attraction is real as such, and you have to stop acting out of it.

155
00:13:34,349 --> 00:13:38,031
[SPEAKER_05]: And one way of acting out of it is trying to replace it with a subha or whatever else.

156
00:13:38,051 --> 00:13:39,172
[SPEAKER_05]: No, you need to understand it.

157
00:13:39,953 --> 00:13:47,137
[SPEAKER_05]: So, you will not see what lies at the other end of subha, if you're too preoccupied in trying to get rid of it.

158
00:13:49,609 --> 00:14:02,604
[SPEAKER_05]: Try directing out towards it, and get, you know, free from that pressure that imposes over you, or, eh... distracting yourself with other thoughts, just so that... until this disappears, because you can't deal with it.

159
00:14:04,547 --> 00:14:11,051
[SPEAKER_05]: Precepts are there to protect you, so you don't actually... if you can't deal with it mentally, so you don't act out, okay.

160
00:14:11,791 --> 00:14:25,958
[SPEAKER_05]: Endure the pressure of the pull, so that next time you see something that used to pull you, there will be no underlying tendency, like basically waiting to pounce on you, because you actually saw it as ugly, as unwanted.

161
00:14:26,959 --> 00:14:28,500
[SPEAKER_05]: You saw it as burning embers.

162
00:14:30,661 --> 00:14:32,282
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, that's the... so yeah, he's...

163
00:14:33,682 --> 00:14:43,947
[SPEAKER_03]: Concentrate, concentrate, concentrate, concentrate.

164
00:14:44,027 --> 00:14:45,928
[SPEAKER_05]: Ah, super, super, super.

165
00:14:46,528 --> 00:14:46,728
[SPEAKER_05]: Why?

166
00:14:47,369 --> 00:14:48,649
[SPEAKER_05]: Because you don't want to deal with the pain.

167
00:14:48,889 --> 00:14:50,250
[SPEAKER_05]: Because there's no problem.

168
00:14:50,750 --> 00:14:51,751
[SPEAKER_05]: You haven't acted out.

169
00:14:53,352 --> 00:14:55,153
[SPEAKER_05]: You're obviously not wanting it mentally.

170
00:14:55,633 --> 00:14:56,793
[SPEAKER_05]: So, you're not delighting in it.

171
00:14:56,833 --> 00:14:58,014
[SPEAKER_05]: So, what's the problem then?

172
00:14:58,154 --> 00:14:59,355
[SPEAKER_05]: Why can't you bear that pain?

173
00:15:00,895 --> 00:15:02,076
[SPEAKER_05]: Oh, because you don't want pain.

174
00:15:03,626 --> 00:15:06,187
[SPEAKER_05]: Which means, by proxy, you still want pleasure.

175
00:15:06,807 --> 00:15:08,048
[SPEAKER_05]: Just not this anymore, sure.

176
00:15:09,489 --> 00:15:18,632
[SPEAKER_05]: So, for as long as you try to, as we said before, as long as you're trying to get rid of the pain, and replace it with pleasure, you will want to have pleasure and not have pain.

177
00:15:19,333 --> 00:15:24,655
[SPEAKER_05]: Thus, you're still very much within sensual domain, on account of you trying to overcome your sensuality.

178
00:15:28,055 --> 00:15:38,819
[SPEAKER_05]: So, don't act out, don't delight in it, but then, when seen, when thought of, see through it, not at the expense of it.

179
00:15:38,839 --> 00:15:44,182
[SPEAKER_03]: Yes, see, and as this simile says, see the danger in it, you know, suppose...

180
00:15:44,202 --> 00:15:47,303
[SPEAKER_03]: So, how does he see, look at sensual pleasures or beauty?

181
00:15:48,635 --> 00:15:53,396
[SPEAKER_03]: Suppose there is a charcoal pit deeper than a man's height, filled with glowing coals.

182
00:15:53,416 --> 00:15:59,158
[SPEAKER_03]: A man would come along, wanting to live, not wanting to die, desiring happiness and averse to suffering.

183
00:15:59,938 --> 00:16:04,380
[SPEAKER_03]: Then two strong men would grab him by both arms and drag him towards that charcoal pit.

184
00:16:05,020 --> 00:16:07,061
[SPEAKER_03]: The man would wriggle his body this way and that.

185
00:16:07,701 --> 00:16:08,321
[SPEAKER_03]: For what reason?

186
00:16:08,421 --> 00:16:14,143
[SPEAKER_03]: Because he knows, I will fall into this charcoal pit and I will thereby meet death or deadly suffering.

187
00:16:14,243 --> 00:16:18,184
[SPEAKER_03]: So, it is dangerous, this thing that's beautiful.

188
00:16:18,996 --> 00:16:33,230
[SPEAKER_05]: Well, ironically enough, when people try to get rid of these sensual perceptions, they're trying to get rid of the pain of recognizing the charcoal pit in them.

189
00:16:34,892 --> 00:16:35,973
[SPEAKER_05]: That's what they're trying to get rid of.

190
00:16:37,566 --> 00:16:42,309
[SPEAKER_05]: So, asubha, asubha, concentrate, don't think about, don't think about, remove the thoughts, remove the thoughts, remove the thoughts.

191
00:16:42,669 --> 00:16:47,672
[SPEAKER_05]: It's basically just stop showing me the pain of the charcoal pit, because I can't bear it.

192
00:16:47,933 --> 00:16:48,153
[SPEAKER_05]: Why?

193
00:16:48,353 --> 00:16:49,834
[SPEAKER_05]: Because it's still very painful.

194
00:16:49,994 --> 00:16:51,074
[SPEAKER_05]: Yes, this thing is painful.

195
00:16:51,335 --> 00:16:53,776
[SPEAKER_03]: And if I go in this direction, that is dangerous.

196
00:16:54,216 --> 00:16:56,378
[SPEAKER_05]: Leave that pain of the pressure.

197
00:16:56,598 --> 00:16:58,399
[SPEAKER_05]: Don't act out by body, by speech.

198
00:16:58,579 --> 00:17:00,801
[SPEAKER_05]: Don't mentally proliferate further.

199
00:17:01,521 --> 00:17:06,265
[SPEAKER_05]: But then, thoughts that are there, that are pressuring you, painful, that's what you train in regard to.

200
00:17:06,565 --> 00:17:11,349
[SPEAKER_05]: And then, you realize, oh, yeah, this pain, I'm just habitually trying to sort of evade.

201
00:17:11,630 --> 00:17:11,770
No.

202
00:17:12,911 --> 00:17:14,912
[SPEAKER_05]: That is the pain of these things.

203
00:17:16,594 --> 00:17:17,594
[SPEAKER_05]: And I'm not there now.

204
00:17:18,635 --> 00:17:21,418
[SPEAKER_05]: Because I've already been withdrawn by not acting towards.

205
00:17:22,118 --> 00:17:26,322
[SPEAKER_05]: Now, I just needed to stop resisting that pain to see where that pain truly belongs.

206
00:17:27,401 --> 00:17:28,062
[SPEAKER_05]: It's not to me.

207
00:17:28,242 --> 00:17:29,744
[SPEAKER_05]: It's not because of my sense restraint.

208
00:17:29,844 --> 00:17:30,625
[SPEAKER_05]: It's not because of mine.

209
00:17:31,006 --> 00:17:33,068
[SPEAKER_05]: It's because that's what sensuality is.

210
00:17:33,128 --> 00:17:34,110
[SPEAKER_05]: A charcoal pit.

211
00:17:35,251 --> 00:17:39,316
[SPEAKER_05]: And then, you won't have to be restraining yourself anymore, because you wouldn't want to go into a charcoal pit.

212
00:17:44,663 --> 00:17:46,145
[SPEAKER_04]: So, seeing the danger...

213
00:17:47,958 --> 00:17:59,742
[SPEAKER_03]: And then how, from that point, how has a bhikkhu comprehended a way of living whereby evil, unwholesome states of greed and sadness or grief do not flow in upon him?

214
00:18:03,963 --> 00:18:04,163
[SPEAKER_05]: How?

215
00:18:06,424 --> 00:18:14,666
[SPEAKER_03]: What will be the way of living that he has discovered now, that will keep him safe?

216
00:18:18,673 --> 00:18:20,574
[SPEAKER_05]: And make him cross to the other shore.

217
00:18:25,596 --> 00:18:26,577
[SPEAKER_04]: A noble path.

218
00:18:26,637 --> 00:18:28,738
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, I was going to say, what else?

219
00:18:29,278 --> 00:18:31,299
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, yeah.

220
00:18:31,479 --> 00:18:34,480
[SPEAKER_03]: But what you said in the beginning is, well, heedfulness.

221
00:18:34,500 --> 00:18:35,781
[SPEAKER_03]: Heedfulness, yeah.

222
00:18:36,121 --> 00:18:38,422
[SPEAKER_02]: Heedfulness in regard to the noble path.

223
00:18:38,962 --> 00:18:40,983
[SPEAKER_03]: So, this is to how I should live now.

224
00:18:41,944 --> 00:18:42,464
[SPEAKER_05]: Very careful.

225
00:18:42,484 --> 00:18:44,845
[SPEAKER_05]: For my own welfare, which is what I always wanted.

226
00:18:45,819 --> 00:18:53,183
[SPEAKER_05]: That's why I was seeking sensuality and safety and comfort and, you know, identity in the world.

227
00:18:53,223 --> 00:18:55,544
[SPEAKER_05]: It's because I thought that was for my own welfare.

228
00:18:55,685 --> 00:18:57,265
[SPEAKER_05]: I thought it was for my well-being.

229
00:18:58,126 --> 00:19:00,187
[SPEAKER_05]: Now I recognize, no, that was all a trap.

230
00:19:01,248 --> 00:19:01,448
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah.

231
00:19:02,668 --> 00:19:04,630
[SPEAKER_05]: I still want the well-being of myself.

232
00:19:04,670 --> 00:19:05,610
[SPEAKER_05]: That's still a valid thing.

233
00:19:06,010 --> 00:19:08,632
[SPEAKER_05]: But now you recognize where that welfare truly is.

234
00:19:08,752 --> 00:19:09,832
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, it still remains you.

235
00:19:10,613 --> 00:19:14,135
[SPEAKER_05]: So, the criteria remains, but now you go very differently about it.

236
00:19:15,525 --> 00:19:16,506
[SPEAKER_03]: And the simile.

237
00:19:16,726 --> 00:19:18,907
[SPEAKER_03]: Suppose a man would enter a thorny forest.

238
00:19:19,548 --> 00:19:24,911
[SPEAKER_03]: There would be thorns in front of him, thorns behind, thorns to the left, thorns to the right, thorns below him, thorns above him.

239
00:19:25,612 --> 00:19:27,273
[SPEAKER_03]: He would go forward mindfully.

240
00:19:27,973 --> 00:19:31,035
[SPEAKER_03]: He would go back mindfully, thinking, may no thorn stab me.

241
00:19:31,716 --> 00:19:38,180
[SPEAKER_03]: So, too, whatever in the world has a pleasing and agreeable nature is called a thorn in the noble one's discipline.

242
00:19:38,800 --> 00:19:40,822
[SPEAKER_05]: So, don't stab yourself on it.

243
00:19:41,162 --> 00:19:43,824
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, and just... there is thorns.

244
00:19:43,844 --> 00:19:44,364
[SPEAKER_03]: Be careful.

245
00:19:47,284 --> 00:19:58,751
[SPEAKER_03]: And then, having understood this way of living, one should then make... understand restraint and non-restraint.

246
00:20:00,172 --> 00:20:00,352
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah.

247
00:20:00,452 --> 00:20:01,332
[SPEAKER_03]: You should know the difference.

248
00:20:01,352 --> 00:20:03,854
[SPEAKER_03]: Like, okay, so now, how do I be careful in this?

249
00:20:04,814 --> 00:20:12,599
[SPEAKER_05]: You understand the extent of restraint and what it leads to, and the extent of non-restraint and what it leads to.

250
00:20:13,451 --> 00:20:15,772
[SPEAKER_05]: Not just an immediate, oh, I restrain, I don't restrain.

251
00:20:15,832 --> 00:20:16,172
[SPEAKER_05]: No.

252
00:20:16,412 --> 00:20:22,575
[SPEAKER_05]: Life, a mode of being of restraint, leads away from this, and he understands it.

253
00:20:23,075 --> 00:20:25,496
[SPEAKER_05]: He's not just taking it on faith to practice a restraint.

254
00:20:25,556 --> 00:20:32,199
[SPEAKER_05]: He sees a direct result of restraint, and direct result of non-restraint.

255
00:20:32,339 --> 00:20:34,060
[SPEAKER_05]: Simultaneously there, he sees it.

256
00:20:35,360 --> 00:20:39,422
[SPEAKER_05]: And it's not even a matter of choice by that point, what he will choose and do.

257
00:20:41,511 --> 00:20:42,572
[SPEAKER_03]: So, what does he understand?

258
00:20:42,792 --> 00:20:43,612
[SPEAKER_03]: There's no restraint.

259
00:20:44,353 --> 00:20:46,195
[SPEAKER_03]: Here, having...

260
00:20:46,215 --> 00:20:47,816
[SPEAKER_03]: I've just translated a bit different.

261
00:20:47,856 --> 00:20:54,882
[SPEAKER_03]: Here, having perceived an object with the senses, Bhikkhu is intent upon a pleasing form and repelled by a displeasing form.

262
00:20:55,803 --> 00:21:00,987
[SPEAKER_03]: He lives without having set up recollection of the body, with a limited, narrow mind.

263
00:21:01,828 --> 00:21:08,954
[SPEAKER_03]: And in that way, he does not know a liberated mind or a liberation through wisdom, whereby those evil, unwholesome states completely cease.

264
00:21:10,708 --> 00:21:14,931
[SPEAKER_03]: So, he understands what restraint is.

265
00:21:14,951 --> 00:21:16,552
[SPEAKER_03]: Restraint is not being intent upon.

266
00:21:16,572 --> 00:21:19,875
[SPEAKER_03]: Restraint does not mean not having eyes and not seeing things.

267
00:21:19,895 --> 00:21:27,960
[SPEAKER_05]: Restraint means... Understanding the restraint or non-restraint means understanding where the problem of lust and non-lust

268
00:21:39,446 --> 00:21:40,526
[SPEAKER_05]: Where the problem of lust is.

269
00:21:42,367 --> 00:21:48,888
[SPEAKER_05]: And it's in grasping the beautiful signs, not getting rid of the beautiful things in the world.

270
00:21:49,168 --> 00:22:00,491
[SPEAKER_05]: So, if I keep attending to these signs that remain present, but I choose to then attend to them further, mind of lust will increase.

271
00:22:00,931 --> 00:22:01,931
[SPEAKER_05]: And he fully understands that.

272
00:22:02,432 --> 00:22:03,812
[SPEAKER_05]: The animal will go wilder.

273
00:22:03,932 --> 00:22:04,552
[SPEAKER_05]: I attained it.

274
00:22:04,872 --> 00:22:05,832
[SPEAKER_05]: It almost listens to me.

275
00:22:06,453 --> 00:22:07,933
[SPEAKER_05]: Now I'm letting it go wild again.

276
00:22:09,074 --> 00:22:21,762
[SPEAKER_05]: If I don't attend to these things that are still present there, I'm not trying to get rid of them either, but I simply don't attend to them, the animal will remain calm, and the mind will turn toward liberation, not toward lust.

277
00:22:23,323 --> 00:22:24,844
[SPEAKER_05]: And it's directly seen and understood.

278
00:22:30,207 --> 00:22:33,649
[SPEAKER_03]: But then, so he lives in such a way, his restraint.

279
00:22:35,692 --> 00:22:43,519
[SPEAKER_03]: Then, occasionally, due to a lapse of memory, mindfulness, evil and wholesome intentions connected with the defilements arise in him.

280
00:22:44,620 --> 00:22:52,246
[SPEAKER_03]: Slow might be the arising of his recollection and his remembering, but then he quickly abandons the spells and puts an end to them.

281
00:22:52,266 --> 00:22:57,330
[SPEAKER_03]: So, even though it does arise again, you know, he is being mindful.

282
00:22:57,350 --> 00:23:03,255
[SPEAKER_05]: So, see, he puts, he dispels, he gets rid of them, he puts an end to them.

283
00:23:03,576 --> 00:23:03,856
[SPEAKER_05]: How?

284
00:23:05,640 --> 00:23:14,124
[SPEAKER_05]: By slowly, slow arising of the context of the mindfulness, of what is peril, what is danger, what is sensory strength.

285
00:23:14,605 --> 00:23:16,406
[SPEAKER_05]: Not by... he gets rid of them.

286
00:23:16,506 --> 00:23:18,727
[SPEAKER_05]: He gets rid of them by thinking about something else.

287
00:23:19,467 --> 00:23:19,627
[SPEAKER_04]: No.

288
00:23:20,148 --> 00:23:24,630
[SPEAKER_05]: He gets rid of them by recreating

289
00:23:26,055 --> 00:23:29,998
[SPEAKER_05]: Recollecting the bigger picture that he has discerned beforehand.

290
00:23:30,158 --> 00:23:30,939
[SPEAKER_05]: That's exactly.

291
00:23:31,319 --> 00:23:40,446
[SPEAKER_05]: Basically, as the other simile in the Sutta says, like, you heat up a pan and as the drops of water fall, they will evaporate immediately if the pan is hot.

292
00:23:42,267 --> 00:23:48,452
[SPEAKER_05]: So, he gets rid of them by heating the pan, not by catching the drops of water and preventing it from falling onto the pan.

293
00:23:49,012 --> 00:23:49,153
[SPEAKER_05]: No.

294
00:23:49,333 --> 00:23:50,474
[SPEAKER_05]: Keep the pan hot.

295
00:23:51,134 --> 00:23:52,255
[SPEAKER_05]: Nothing can stick to it.

296
00:23:53,376 --> 00:23:54,977
[SPEAKER_05]: No water can soak it.

297
00:23:57,237 --> 00:24:01,020
[SPEAKER_03]: Oh, that's that's the samādhi, actually.

298
00:24:01,040 --> 00:24:14,351
[SPEAKER_04]: Just as a man let two or three drops of water fall onto an iron plate, heated for a whole day, slow might be the falling of the drops of water, but then they would quickly vaporize and vanish.

299
00:24:18,794 --> 00:24:21,717
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's almost finished, yeah?

300
00:24:22,057 --> 00:24:22,998
[SPEAKER_03]: Thus a bhikkhu has

301
00:24:25,577 --> 00:24:31,945
[SPEAKER_03]: Thus Bhikkhu has comprehended a way of living whereby evil and wholesome states of greed and sadness do not flow in upon him.

302
00:24:34,569 --> 00:24:41,438
[SPEAKER_03]: And when he is living in such a way, various other people might invite him to accept wealth.

303
00:24:42,722 --> 00:24:44,703
[SPEAKER_03]: There might be some options available.

304
00:24:46,404 --> 00:24:48,025
[SPEAKER_03]: Some praise, gain, fame.

305
00:24:48,946 --> 00:24:51,668
[SPEAKER_03]: Come, good man, why let these robes weigh you down?

306
00:24:54,470 --> 00:24:57,211
[SPEAKER_03]: Why roam about with a shaven head and a begging bowl?

307
00:24:58,192 --> 00:25:02,415
[SPEAKER_03]: Come, having returned to the lower life, enjoy wealth and do notorious deeds.

308
00:25:04,635 --> 00:25:11,897
[SPEAKER_03]: Indeed, when a bhikkhu is living in that way, it is impossible that he will give up the training and return to the lower life.

309
00:25:14,338 --> 00:25:15,118
[SPEAKER_03]: The simile.

310
00:25:16,038 --> 00:25:21,720
[SPEAKER_03]: Suppose that when the river Ganges slants and slopes and inclines towards the east,

311
00:25:22,432 --> 00:25:31,734
[SPEAKER_03]: And a great crowd of people would come along, bringing a shovel and a basket, thinking, we will make this river Ganges slant and slope and incline towards the west.

312
00:25:32,534 --> 00:25:40,416
[SPEAKER_03]: What do you think, Bhikkhus, would that great crowd of people be able to make the river Ganges slant, slope and incline towards the west?

313
00:25:40,456 --> 00:25:41,216
[SPEAKER_03]: No, Venerable Sir.

314
00:25:42,016 --> 00:25:42,636
[SPEAKER_03]: For what reason?

315
00:25:42,656 --> 00:25:49,878
[SPEAKER_03]: Because the river Ganges slants, slopes and inclines towards the east, and it's not easy to make it slant and incline towards the west.

316
00:25:50,758 --> 00:25:55,362
[SPEAKER_03]: That crowd, great crowd of people, would only reap fatigue and disappointment.

317
00:25:56,243 --> 00:26:03,669
[SPEAKER_03]: Likewise, it is with the bhikkhu whose mind has for a long time slanted, sloped and inclined towards detachment or seclusion.

318
00:26:04,670 --> 00:26:08,813
[SPEAKER_03]: Thus, it is impossible that he will give up the training and return to the low life.

319
00:26:08,833 --> 00:26:11,435
[SPEAKER_03]: So, it's completely protected, really.

320
00:26:12,176 --> 00:26:19,142
[SPEAKER_03]: No matter how many people or pressures, external possibilities, invitations,

321
00:26:22,858 --> 00:26:24,774
[SPEAKER_03]: They'll just fall flat anyway.

